RECENTERING DOMESTIC WORK : THE MEDIEVAL JURISTS - Despite a certain marginality in legal-historical scholarship, “domestic work” was anything but peripheral in late-medieval legal systems. The inherited Roman notion of work, “operae”, occurs chiefly in the Digest title De operis libertorum (D. 38.1), where it is treated as an asset deeply inherent to the Roman household. Drawing on a body of late-medieval scholastic writings known as the distinctiones operarum, this paper asks what “domestic work” meant for late-medieval jurists and what role it played in the economies of their own times. It shows that domestic work did not simply denote an activity intrinsic to the house. Rather, it reveals the latter as a far broader institution for valuing and regulating work at large. 
Edited with Claude (Anthropic)

Panel: Care work. Counting and understanding care work and domestic work in the societies of the past (WP: Labour and Family Economy), 6th European Labour History Network Conference. University of Barcelona - 18 June 2026, Barcelona, Spain.
